3:<meta name="description" content="Version 5: your comments of 2 November applied. Each rule and each fix is one block that holds its new text, its old text, and what it changes and why, with only its provenance outside the Select range. Seven items land now, nine are redrafted, ten wait for your read, two fixes are dropped.">
286:<ul><li><span class="add">Send a follow-up question to the worker that already holds its material, with <code>SendMessage</code>, also when it has finished: it goes on from its transcript and does not search again.</span> If a worker waits on the curator between tasks, run it as a headless session and resume it within the hour (<code>--resume &lt;id&gt;</code>).</li></ul>
291:<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> Today the skill names this reuse only for a long writing pass. With the change, every follow-up question goes to the worker that already holds its material, a finished one too, which goes on from its transcript and pays for no new search.</p></div>
420:<ul><li>Keep your context for decisions. Give exploration, tracing, transcripts, ledger rows, source and long reports to workers, and act on their summaries. Read a worker's final message yourself.</li></ul>
538:<p>“Why is it third time here? … The point is the coordinator after light boot does not forget the delegation procedure. It must load delegating always. So, for example, we have a script that runs after the compaction. If that script instructs the coordinator session to load the delegating.md part, we can leave it out from the boot description or just have it once.”</p></blockquote>
635:<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> The part named how to set a worker's tier, but not its effort, and “it” and “large” were unclear. With the change, a bullet of its own names the effort setting in each tool. Neither script that starts a headless session passes an effort, so the bullet names no flag for one.</p></div>
667:<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> The old sentence did not say which sentences “this” covers, or how to read Fable's version. With the change, it covers the rules on Fable's text, and the script that prints this session's model shows the version.</p></div>
715:<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> The old sentence could read as a fact about the batch script or as an order, and “until the switch-over” as until it starts or until it lands. With the change, it states a fact, which holds until the switch-over lands. The batch script's gate runs both counts in every batch.</p></div>
726:<ul><li>If a batch's gather was killed mid-work, resume the <span class="add">run, and leave</span> the main tree as the gather left it. <span class="add">On the resume, the batch script's <code>recoverGather()</code> gives the gather a</span> prompt <span class="add">that</span> takes the uncommitted changes as its earlier attempt's work. <span class="add">You write no prompt. The check for a clean tree is the gather's first start's only.</span></li></ul>
731:<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> The coordinator could not tell whether it must write the gather's prompt, as it does for the commit stage, and a resume on a tree with changes seemed to break the gather's check for a clean tree. With the change, the batch script writes the prompt, and the check holds only at the gather's first start. The writer checked both in the script.</p></div>
